{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789380330,"nonce":"mv+u52QDKCXbUSJ1dWkId87OY0g=","title":"OSR helper reads a sub-word live-in as a word","message":"A tier-1 OSR helper recovers each live-in from the frame with a load\ntyped from the backend's type cache, falling back to I64 when the type\nis not cached. The cache is filled from phi types and a few instruction\nkinds, so a `bool` live-in (a compare decided before the loop and\nbranched on inside it, whose Binary instruction is typed at the operand\nwidth) is loaded as eight bytes when the function has no bool phi. The\nprobe stored one byte, so the other seven are the next slot or stack\ngarbage, and the branch goes the wrong way after the transfer.\n\nSeen as `bench_op_overload.py` raising AttributeError on `a.x` after\n`Vec3$new` started inlining into the loop: the hoisted `a is None`\ncheck is such a live-in, and the new frame shape put nonzero bytes\nafter it. With `ZYNTAX_DISABLE_OSR=1` the program is correct.\n\nFix: the helper pre-translates every live-in type into the cache, and\nthe probe converts an integer held at another width to the slot's\nwidth before storing it. Regression test\n`the_tier1_helper_reads_a_byte_live_in_as_a_byte` in\n`crates/compiler/tests/osr_tier_transfer.rs`, which fails on the\nprevious code.","files":null}]}